this is one of 37 labs in india where  scientists are using a process called  genome sequencing to decode the genetic  information in covet 19 to learn more  about the virus the samples of every  positive case in north and central delhi  are kept in this freezer at nearly minus  80 degrees celsius  scientists show us a device called the  flow cell which collects the samples  it's then inserted into a machine that  reveals their genetic code the director  of india's biggest covert 19 hospital  says they have their best minds  analyzing the results to see how the  virus changes into new variants and  understand how these changes affect its  characteristics  we have the phd scholars we have the  researchers we have the professors  doctors who are full time working rather  they are working more than 12 hour per  day  allen jp hospital's coronavirus ward is  receiving international passengers  who've tested positive for the virus  upon their arrival the government has  imposed tighter restrictions at  international airports including  mandatory covert 19 tests for arrivals  it's also delayed the resumption of  international commercial flights as  other governments go further and ban  flights from countries which have cases  of omicron economists say the new  variant is already having a global  impact uncertainty leads to slowdown in  investment  and investment is what leads to growth  and to employment generation right now  any kind of uncertainty that increases  affects the financial markets as we saw  the stock markets immediately declined  they may recover a little but as long as  the uncertainty remains the financial  markets will get affected and investment  will get affected you know  sociologists say while many are anxious  about another possible wave governments  must keep the poor in mind as they  decide next steps there are different  kind of people  in this country and in this world and i  think the impact of kovid has been felt  and experienced very differently across  different social categories of people  and i think that's something which we  need to underline not because for some  kind of sociological reasons but also  for policy reasons  the first year of the pandemic pushed  230 million indians into poverty
 

